@* The spirit's colour as a bar down the leading edge of whatever names it — the seat's spirit
   button on the setup screen, the recorded game's player card, the statistics table's row, the
   reference sheet's header, the merge dialog's game list, a person card's usual pick.

   All of them carry the subject's name beside it and most carry a second line under that, and
   they had each picked their own height: h-9 four times, h-7 once, h-3.5 once, and the sheet's
   stretched to its header — with the merge dialog's and the statistics row's spelled out by
   hand rather than reaching for this at all. The ladder below pairs the bar with what it stands
   beside, once, so two bars at one size cannot drift again.

   ColorDot is the same marker where a subject is named on one line and the mark is a tick
   beside the word; this is for the block that leads with it. *@

<span class="@SizeClasses inline-block w-1.5 shrink-0 rounded-full @Class"
      style="background-color: @Color;"></span>

@code {
    /// <summary>The subject's colour — usually <see cref="Games.GameLookups.SpiritColor"/>.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Color { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>How the host lays the bar out — a nudge onto the first line's baseline, say.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>"md" (default) beside a name and its play style, wherever that pair is a row or a
    /// card; "sm" where the same pair is set in small type, as in a sidebar list; "xs" beside a
    /// single line; "full" to take the height of whatever it is laid beside — the reference
    /// sheet's header band, a highlight card's body.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string Size { get; set; } = "md";

    private string SizeClasses => Size switch
    {
        "full" => "self-stretch",
        "sm"   => "h-7",
        "xs"   => "h-3.5",
        _      => "h-9", // "md"
    };
}
